use @piton/belay

from ../../lib/PitonSkillBase import PitonSkillBase

// Shared base for the crux UI prototyping skills. It is abstract, so
// it never compiles or is emitted on its own. It holds the rules for
// saved mockups, numbered variations and the gallery page, so every
// crux-ui skill writes and reads mockups the same way. Shared
// guidance lives in named properties, which inherit cleanly and are
// serialized as sections after each concrete skill's own prompt.
export abstract anchor CruxUiBase extends PitonSkillBase:
    mockupFiles:
        Mockups are saved project files, not throwaway output. Save
        them next to the specification file they belong to, in a folder
        named after that file with a .mockups suffix. For
        spec/surface/Card.pi the folder is spec/surface/Card.mockups.
        Each round gets its own subfolder, round-1, round-2 and so on.
        Inside a round, each variation is one file named by its number,
        1.html, 2.html, 3.html, and the round's gallery is index.html.
        The folder also has a top-level index.html that lists every
        round, links to each round's gallery, and marks the variation
        picked in each round. Never overwrite or renumber an earlier
        round. A new round is always a new folder.

        Piton reads only .pi files, so the mockups never affect piton
        check or piton build. If the project tethers its source root as
        a package, the mockups travel with it, which is intended.

    referencingMockups:
        The specification must point at its mockups. Give the anchor
        that describes the UI a mockups property. In it, name the
        mockups folder index and the picked variation of the latest
        round, as paths relative to the project root, where
        piton.config.pi lives, such as
        spec/surface/Card.mockups/round-2/3.html. Add one plain
        sentence per pick saying what it settled. Use project-root
        paths because a Markdown link written in prose is plain text
        that Piton never rewrites, so a relative link would break in
        the compiled reference tree. Keep the property in step with
        each new pick. Run piton format, piton check and piton build
        after editing it.

        A mockup illustrates the specification. It never replaces it.
        Every behaviour a pick settles, such as layout order, what
        shows when a list is empty, or what a control does, is also
        written as plain requirements in the anchor. Someone who never
        opens the mockup must still be able to build the UI from the
        specification.

    variations:
        Number variations 1, 2, 3 and so on, in the order they appear
        in the gallery. Use the same number in the file name, in the
        gallery card, and in everything you say about it, so the user
        can pick one by number alone.

        Make the variations genuinely different. Vary what matters for
        the open question of the round, such as layout, information
        order, density, navigation, or how an interaction works. Never
        make variations that differ only in colour or font size. Give
        each one a short name and one sentence on the idea behind it.

        Build each variation from the project, not from generic
        placeholders. Follow the design rules the specification already
        has, such as palette, spacing, type, icons, tone, accessibility
        and language rules. Use realistic content shaped like the
        project's real data, never lorem ipsum. Show the states the
        specification cares about, such as empty, loading, error, long
        text, a second language, or a narrow screen, when they matter
        for the round.

        Each variation file is a single self-contained HTML page with
        inline CSS. Use JavaScript only to show an interaction that
        cannot be seen without it. Load nothing from the network, so
        the files open offline and look the same years later. A mockup
        is a picture of the UI, not the implementation, so never write
        it into the application code and never copy it there.

    gallery:
        Each round has one gallery page, index.html in the round
        folder, that shows every variation of the round at once. It is
        also a single self-contained HTML page that loads nothing from
        the network. It shows the round number and the question the
        round explores. Then it shows one card per variation with the
        number large and clear, the short name, the one-sentence idea,
        and a live preview of the variation file in an iframe.

        The gallery lets the user dive into any variation. Clicking a
        card, or pressing its number key, opens a large view of that
        variation inside the gallery. The large view has a width
        switch for wide, tablet and 375px phone sizes, previous and
        next controls, a link that opens the variation file on its
        own, and Escape to return to the grid. In a later round, the
        gallery also links back to the variation it grew from.

        After writing a round, show the gallery. Give the user its path
        relative to the working directory, and open it with whatever
        preview the host actually provides, such as a browser tool or
        the system's file opener. Never assume a particular tool
        exists. If iframes do not load from the file system in the
        user's browser, suggest serving the mockups folder with a
        simple local static server and give the exact command.

    picking:
        The user picks a variation by telling you its number, such as
        2, or I like 2. They may also combine, such as 2 with the
        header from 3, ask for changes to one, ask to dive deeper into
        one, reject all of them, or ask for a different number of
        variations. Treat a combination or a change as the starting
        point for the next round, not as a pick of an existing file.
        Never pick for the user, and never treat silence as a pick.

        Once a pick is made, record it. Mark it in the top-level
        mockups index, update the mockups property in the
        specification, and write what the pick settled as plain
        requirements in the anchor.
